is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router_core_2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF routing on the core layer. The OSPF daemon (ospfd) is not running on router_core_2, which is the only L3 uplink for Pod 2’s distribution switch.

Mechanism: With OSPF down on router_core_2, switch_dist_2_1 (Pod 2’s default gateway) has no OSPF adjacency to the core and therefore learns no routes beyond its directly connected VLANs. When host_2_1_1_1 sends traffic to 10.200.0.x or 10.1.1.0/24, switch_dist_2_1 cannot forward it and returns ICMP Destination Net Unreachable, while still answering local pings (gateway reachability intact).

Localization and evidence:
- From host_2_1_1_1 to host_1_1_1_1: “From 10.2.1.1 Destination Net Unreachable” (gateway is switch_dist_2_1).
- switch_dist_2_1 routing table contains only connected networks (10.2.1.0/24, 172.16.0.8/31), no routes to 10.1.1.0/24 or 10.200.0.0/24.
- switch_dist_2_1 shows no OSPF neighbors.
- Direct evidence on implicated device: router_core_2 reports “ospfd is not running”.

Root cause: The OSPF daemon on router_core_2 is stopped, breaking Pod 2’s routing to the rest of the network.

Corrective fix: Restart and enable OSPF on router_core_2 (e.g., ensure FRR/ospfd service is running and enabled). After ospfd is up, verify OSPF neighbors between router_core_2 and switch_dist_2_1 on 172.16.0.8/31, confirm routes to 10.1.1.0/24 and 10.200.0.0/24 appear on switch_dist_2_1, and re-test reachability from host_2_1_1_1 to 10.200.0.x and 10.1.1.2. Pod 1 remains unaffected as it routes via router_core_1 and router_core_3. 
=== END ===